#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f64efad-73ef-5caa-b38b-060d24ce13cf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial Whose Terms Have a Common Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11615e8c-79b7-5430-8245-0a55f42d7861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(r-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557903} {:answer/id #uuid "564d466f-3e14-571e-98fa-b5a61a8cfbed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(r-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558028} {:answer/id #uuid "af67a4bf-7608-52ba-b8bb-4cfbeb581cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(2r-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558215} {:answer/id #uuid "afb197b9-ef77-55c0-8d73-cfe31f69e11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(4r-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558216} {:answer/id #uuid "d5417484-3bdb-5d4d-ae0d-d6f710f8b199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(2r-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af67a4bf-7608-52ba-b8bb-4cfbeb581cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(2r-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558215} :answer-field/id #uuid "c99dfe19-b0ba-52de-a707-b226b7df9dfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b4b9fe6-eb97-5c93-8f7d-fda1228ca372" :question/math-academy-id "q-111641" :question/problem "Factor the expression $16{r}^{2}-16r+4.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $4.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{r}^{2}-16r+4 & = \\\\ 4(4{r}^{2}-4r+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{r}^{2}-4r+1 & = \\\\ (2r)^{2}-4r+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ (2r)^{2}-2\\cdot (2r\\cdot 1)+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ (2r-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(4{r}^{2}-4r+1) & = \\\\ 4(2r-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69a3b7a7-1042-511a-aaff-8b0911eae499" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03fdc9f9-e821-5130-94c5-b047cb92adcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557877} {:answer/id #uuid "7dcf9040-4fc0-56e1-866d-757572273d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558111} {:answer/id #uuid "a0646697-4c1e-5318-a5ed-5b39405c2f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x+4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558183} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f57c5f-876f-5134-b6a1-aefa35bdd924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558197} {:answer/id #uuid "fb679a03-40d9-58cc-afc5-baf751a42497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7f57c5f-876f-5134-b6a1-aefa35bdd924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558197} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e236f4-214d-5a90-ab31-d0cd6e1daf09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82da910d-da60-52ba-aecb-7ff9ed90f208" :question/math-academy-id "q-31979" :question/problem "Factor $9{x}^{2}-12x+4$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2}=(a-b)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}-12x+4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-12x+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(6x)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(6x)$ as a product of $3x$ and $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x)^{2}-2(3x\\cdot 2)+(2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=3x$ and $b=2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b67de190-3b32-506e-b375-bd18d4543bf6" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f927e8-fd04-5b24-9051-6b5a918ad64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(3x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557925} {:answer/id #uuid "32f2a486-695a-5441-9417-59951b7b6c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557973} {:answer/id #uuid "4c6361cb-48a2-5f00-a113-53b05b147d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{(3x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558013} {:answer/id #uuid "be3f0de2-0bc3-54c5-b294-c07f2c0dc17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(3x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558247} {:answer/id #uuid "ef63b783-49af-546d-8cf4-f1476656f81b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(3x+\\frac{4}{9})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be3f0de2-0bc3-54c5-b294-c07f2c0dc17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(3x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558247} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b7c1f54-810f-55b1-ab45-181c23bc394b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6884ae1e-aa7f-516d-8c30-2f343ce00f89" :question/math-academy-id "q-79010" :question/problem "Factor $-9{x}^{2}+4x-\\frac{4}{9}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{x}^{2}+4x-\\frac{4}{9} & = \\\\ -(9{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{4}{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference  formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{4}{9} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-4x+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ (3x)^{2}-2\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3x)+{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(3x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(9{x}^{2}-4x+\\frac{4}{9}) & = \\\\ -{(3x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
